# port-patches.sh: glm/r8b @ cf4dde927470 over glm/zig-engine-fixes-on-186 @ e027c49cd06d (commits 95526fcaa650..863b5f903e44); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
glm_cuda: MTP drafts with the prompt cache (one stream): the head absorbs one row behind the target and kept states carry its section; TF_GLM_MTP_DIGEST

The MTP head is one per engine and kept states had no MTP section, so
cacheFor turned the cache off whenever a head was loaded (also beside
DFlash2, TF_GLM_MTP=1, where it is never fed). Turning it on as it stood
would have been wrong: prefillWith set prompt_at = 0 from any begin, pairing
a continuation's hidden rows with the prompt's first tokens, and reset the
head's cursor on every pass; and the head absorbed ahead of the target, so
after a chunk ending at a keep point k it had used token k, which is past
the state.

- cuda_mtp_keep (new): the head absorbs one row behind in every pass. A
  target chunk [s, e) feeds head rows [s - 1, e - 1) with tokens [s, e), row
  s - 1 from the tail (the target's row s - 1, the head's pending row), the
  chunk's last row the next tail; the first chunk feeds [0, e - 1). After any
  chunk ending at k the head stands at k - 1 with row k - 1 pending, so a
  resume from k does what the keeping run did next (exactness rests on the
  64-grid cut, not on the head's chunk invariance). The cursor is checked
  before every chunk (BadMtpCursor), on a resume or live continuation
  (MtpNotAtResume) and at a keep; TF_GLM_MTP_DIGEST's digest of what the
  head's next pass reads.
- cuda_snapshot: an optional MTP section after the others (withMtp): the
  head's latent rows [0, at - 1), their pools, its two index rings sized from
  the head's own ring, the 8 KB tail; draftBytes() for the DFlash2 section.
- cuda_mtp.Head: absorb (the tail row staged first, the next tail taken
  before the head's layers reuse the prompt buffers), its own ring on the
  prompt path, plane(), slot; resetCopies (the cursor is cuda_mtp_keep's).
- cuda_engine: mtpHead()/mtpRing() (the head only when it drafts);
  prefillWith: prompt_at = begin, enter() from 0 or from where the head
  stands, the head alone restored when the target's rows stand at the state
  but the head does not; takeSnap/restoreSnap copy the section (mtp_len =
  at - 1, MtpMismatch either way); TF_GLM_MTP_DIGEST=1 logs the head's digest
  per rank at every keep, restore and prompt end and each round's drafts.
- cuda_native: the prompt cache runs with MTP drafts; spill v1 stays off with
  them (logged); spill v2's SpillV2WithMtp and KV sharding's refusal stay.

Switches: none new besides TF_GLM_MTP_DIGEST (off). DFlash2 or no drafts:
unchanged (no section, the same calls). MTP drafts with the cache off: the
head's prompt chunks move one row ([s - 1, e - 1)), so its bits and possibly
its acceptance move; the replies do not (drafts are verified exactly).

Tests (host): a fake head recording (row, token, hidden row): cold prompts
absorb every row once; keep + resume (same and fresh head, another prompt
with the prefix) absorbs what the keeping run did; a refused save goes on
from the live head; moved, drafted and other-slot heads and out-of-order
chunks refused; the digest; the section's sizes and round trip; sections
only when the head drafts; save, restore and live_from refusals before any
device call; spill v1 off with MTP. The keep + resume and refused-save tests
failed against the old absorption first.

Measured on the pair: not run yet (the GPU check compares digests and draft
lines between cold and resumed runs, since greedy exactness cannot see a
wrong head state).

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo); the MTP tail kept one
row short follows the flashnext engine and the Python GLM engine's kept
states (mtp_len, pending).
Apply from the checkout root with: git apply 0025-zig-glm-mtp-cache.patch

